{"parentUuid":"ef563fd1-acc1-4a0c-894e-4606d9ff3055","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c7a9c836-fcfa-483d-8466-444dc60bbf86","agentId":"a6d05a19c340d22c4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01L4xmbcSxZahqBukGiTLr72","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT shared_tree infeasible UNSAT proof prove optimal large search space\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"How does it work? - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/07_under_the_hood.html\"},{\"title\":\"cpmpy.solvers.ortools — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/cp_optimizer/_modules/cpmpy/solvers/ortools.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT: Infeasibility Subsolvers\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/Tz8ovW6cZtY\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT 9.7.2996 Sometimes feasible, sometimes infeasible · Issue #3938 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3938\"},{\"title\":\"Proven Infeasible CP SAT\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/Cttn-RG-X7g\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding CP-SAT: Constraint Programming Meets SAT Solving | by Jonas Poelmans | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@jonaspoelmans120/understanding-cp-sat-constraint-programming-meets-sat-solving-b137dddb943c\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding the Log - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/understanding_the_log.html\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about CP-SAT and its handling of infeasibility, UNSAT proofs, and optimization:\n\n## CP-SAT and Infeasibility Proofs\n\nThe CP-SAT solver returns statuses like OPTIMAL, FEASIBLE, INFEASIBLE, MODEL_INVALID, or UNKNOWN to indicate the solution state. When a problem is determined to be infeasible, UNKNOWN (0) status means no solution was found, and no infeasibility proof is available.\n\n## Search Space and Shared Tree\n\nCP-SAT uses a sophisticated parallel search strategy with a shared tree structure. The log shows \"Clauses shared\" statistics that track how many clauses are shared among different subsolvers, such as 'core': 137, 'default_lp': 1, 'lb_tree_search': 4, etc. This shared clause mechanism allows multiple subsolvers to benefit from learned knowledge across the search space.\n\n## Proving Optimality in Large Search Spaces\n\nThe linear relaxation is used for detecting infeasibility, finding better bounds for the objective and variables, and also for making branching decisions. For proving optimality when dealing with large search spaces, one strategy is to have a candidate optimal solution with objective value U and a lower bound L, then add the constraint L ≤ z ≤ U-1 to show infeasibility and thus prove the optimal value.\n\n## Multiple Subsolvers for Infeasibility\n\nCP-SAT offers multiple subsolvers for handling infeasibility, including 'core', 'max_lp', 'probing', 'lb_tree_search', and 'reduced_costs'.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"5ae3a321-2175-414b-8b14-ffb07ad4a43a","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:01:28.892Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ef563fd1-acc1-4a0c-894e-4606d9ff3055","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
